-- sudo lsof -i :8888
#Component	Meaning
#sudo	Superuser Do. This executes the command with root privileges. This is often necessary because lsof might need elevated permissions to see information about processes owned by other users or the operating system.
#lsof	List Open Files. This is the core utility that lists all open files and the processes that opened them. In Unix-like systems, almost everything (including network connections) is treated as a file.
#-i	Internet (or network) files. This option restricts the output to list only network file listings.
#:8888	Port number. This is an additional filter that specifies the particular TCP or UDP port you are interested in. In this case, it restricts the output to processes that have a network connection (listening or established) on port 8888.


